<Section id="packing" title="1. Packing the Discs by Genre">

Our grid gave every song the same square. But songs aren't really a flat list — they cluster: many songs share an **artist**, and many artists share a **genre**. A **circle-packing** layout shows that nesting directly: each song sits inside its artist's circle, and each artist inside its genre's circle.

Every leaf is the same **vinyl disc** we built — a dark record with four wedges radiating from the centre for popularity, tempo, energy and danceability, and a genre-coloured hub. Here each disc is **sized by popularity**, so the hits grow large while the deep cuts stay small, and every genre is tinted its own colour.

<Bleed max={1200}>
	<PackedCirclesDemo />
</Bleed>

{#snippet sidebar()}

This is exactly the kind of chart that's quick to reach for with an AI assistant: describe the nesting and the leaf design, get a first draft of the D3, then refine it. Try this prompt:

```text
/// file: Prompt to try
I have a dataset of 2010s songs with fields: title, artist, genre,
popularity, bpm, energy, danceability.

Build a D3 circle-packing chart (in Svelte) that groups the songs by
genre, then by artist. Size each leaf circle by popularity, and tint
each genre group with its own colour.

Render every leaf as a small "vinyl disc": a dark circle with four
wedges radiating from the centre — popularity, tempo, energy and
danceability — each wedge's length scaled to its value, plus a small
genre-coloured hub in the middle.
```

> [!Tip] Reading it
> - **Outer circles** are genres; the circles nested inside them are artists.
> - **Disc size** = popularity.
> - **Wedges** = that song's popularity, tempo, energy and danceability.

{/snippet}

</Section>
